   "lesson": "g10m2s3-1",
   "claims": [
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "rule",
     "text": "The exponent laws can also be applied to expressions with rational exponents.",
     "anchor": "b00884",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "We can also apply the exponent laws to expressions with rational exponents.",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "To simplify a product of powers of the same base with rational exponents, add the exponents: in 2x^(1/2) × 4x^(-1/2) the powers of x combine as x^(1/2 - 1/2).",
     "anchor": "WE6",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "8{x}^{\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "When simplifying 2x^(1/2) × 4x^(-1/2), the coefficients 2 and 4 are multiplied to give 8.",
     "anchor": "WE6",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "4{x}^{-\\frac{1}{2}}&=8",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "Adding the exponents 1/2 and -1/2 gives the exponent 0, so 2x^(1/2) × 4x^(-1/2) becomes 8x^0.",
     "anchor": "WE6",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "8{x}^{0}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "A power with exponent 0 is replaced by 1, so 8x^0 = 8(1) = 8.",
     "anchor": "WE6",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "8\\left(1\\right)",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "To simplify a decimal base raised to a rational exponent, write the base as a fraction and simplify it.",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "Write as a fraction and simplify:",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "The decimal 0,008 is written as the fraction 8/1000, so (0,008)^(1/3) = (8/1000)^(1/3).",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "\\frac{8}{1000}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "The fraction 8/1000 is simplified to 1/125, so (8/1000)^(1/3) = (1/125)^(1/3).",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "\\frac{1}{125}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "A rational exponent on a fraction is applied to the numerator and to the denominator separately: (1/5^3)^(1/3) = 1^(1/3) / 5^(3 · 1/3).",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "\\frac{1^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{5^{\\left(3\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-1",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "A power raised to a rational exponent has its exponents multiplied: (5^3)^(1/3) = 5^(3 · 1/3).",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "5^{\\left(3\\cdot\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "Evaluating by this method gives (0,008)^(1/3) = 1/5.",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "\\frac{1}{5}",
     "provenance": "containment"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2",
     "type": "convention",
     "text": "The book writes a decimal number with a comma as the decimal separator, for example 0,008.",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "0,008",
     "provenance": "checked"
    },
    {
     "lo": "lo:g10m2s3-1-2",
     "type": "method",
     "text": "The denominator 125 is rewritten as the prime power 5^3, so (1/125)^(1/3) = (1/5^3)^(1/3).",
     "anchor": "WE7",
     "printed_page": 50,
     "supported": true,
     "teacher_only": false,
     "quote": "5^{3}",
     "provenance": "checked"
    }
   ],
